SUPABASE_URL=###
SUPABASE_KEY=###

# Vision provider for the photo-identification spike (F-03). Server-only secret.
# Create a key at https://openrouter.ai/keys. Local dev goes in .dev.vars; prod via
# `wrangler secret put OPENROUTER_API_KEY`. Optional in the env schema — builds/CI
# proceed without it; the vision service throws loudly only at call time.
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=###

# Playwright e2e account. A confirmed user in the LOCAL Supabase instance — the specs sign in
# through the real /api/auth/signin route (see e2e/auth.setup.ts), so this is not a mock.
# Never point it at a production account: the suite creates and deletes library rows.
# Create one with `npx supabase start`, then seed it from the values below:
#   eval "$(npx supabase status -o env)"
#   SUPABASE_URL="$API_URL" SERVICE_ROLE_KEY="$SERVICE_ROLE_KEY" \
#     E2E_EMAIL=... E2E_PASSWORD=... npm run seed:e2e-user
# Re-running it resets the account's password to whatever you pass, so editing the values here
# and re-seeding is enough — you do not need to reset the stack. It refuses a non-local
# SUPABASE_URL unless ALLOW_REMOTE_SEED=1.
# Fallback, by hand: sign up at http://localhost:4321/auth/signup (Mailpit at
# http://127.0.0.1:54324 catches the confirmation mail).
E2E_EMAIL=###
E2E_PASSWORD=###

# Arms the e2e determinism seam: with this set, a POST /api/identify carrying a matching
# `x-e2e-vision-key` header gets a canned identified read instead of the OpenRouter call, so the
# photo specs run offline, free, and byte-identically (src/lib/services/vision.ts).
# Any non-empty value; treat it as a secret.
#
# It must be IDENTICAL in two places: .dev.vars (the Worker dev server reads it) and .env (the spec
# side reads it to send the header). Wrangler reads .dev.vars first and only falls back to .env /
# process.env when that file is ABSENT, so .dev.vars is the one that decides the server's value.
#
# Astro binds secrets once at worker init, so editing .dev.vars requires a DEV-SERVER RESTART —
# playwright.config.ts reuses an existing server locally and would otherwise keep the stale value.
#
# NEVER set this in production: anyone who learns the value can disable photo identification by
# sending the header. Leaving it unset is the production configuration.
E2E_VISION_STUB_KEY=###

# Sentry error-monitoring sink. Read in exactly one place — the Worker entrypoint
# (sentry.server.config.ts) — so no app code ever touches it.
#
# PRODUCTION ONLY, via `npx wrangler secret put SENTRY_DSN`. Leaving it UNSET is the correct local
# and CI configuration, not a missing one: with no DSN the SDK never initializes and every
# `captureException` degrades to a no-op, so the app behaves exactly as it did before Sentry.
#
# To verify the wiring by hand, drop it into .dev.vars temporarily and RESTART the dev server
# (Astro binds secrets once at worker init), then remove it again when you are done.
#
# NEVER set it in CI: the e2e suite deliberately triggers errors, and those would land in the issue
# stream and burn the free plan's quota.
SENTRY_DSN=###

# Sentry DEPLOY-TIME credentials — a different thing from SENTRY_DSN above, and easy to confuse.
# SENTRY_DSN is a *runtime* Worker secret: where errors get sent. These three are *deploy-time*
# values, used only to upload source maps so a production stack trace shows real src/ paths
# instead of minified output. Nothing at runtime reads them.
#
# They have TWO homes, because there are two deploy paths:
#   1. .env on the deploying developer's machine, for `npm run deploy`. NOT .dev.vars — that file is
#      for Worker runtime bindings.
#   2. The Cloudflare Workers Builds *build environment* (dashboard → Settings → Builds), which
#      auto-deploys every push to main and is therefore the path that matters most. Its deploy
#      command must be `node scripts/deploy-worker.mjs --skip-build`; with a bare `npx wrangler
#      deploy` the upload never runs and production traces silently go back to minified.
# Never commit them.
#
# They are consumed by `npm run deploy` (scripts/deploy-worker.mjs), which is the deploy command
# for this project — not a bare `npx wrangler deploy`. It builds, stamps debug IDs into
# dist/server, deploys, then uploads that directory's maps. dist/server is what actually runs:
# @astrojs/cloudflare marks the Worker `no_bundle`, so wrangler ships those modules verbatim.
#
# SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN: create under Sentry Settings -> Auth Tokens with source-map upload scope
# (project:releases + project:write). Treat as a secret. If any of the three is ABSENT the deploy
# still builds and ships — only the upload is skipped, with a warning — so a deploy never depends
# on having a Sentry account. GitHub Actions CI runs no deploy at all and needs none of these — that
# is CI, not Workers Builds; the two are separate and only the latter deploys.
# SENTRY_ORG / SENTRY_PROJECT: the organization and project slugs. Not secret, but kept here so
# all four Sentry values sit together.
SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN=###
SENTRY_ORG=###
SENTRY_PROJECT=###